{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/newshour/posts/the-us-supreme-court-ruled-5-4-monday-that-states-can-count-mail-in-ballots-that/1485180756810499/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/newshour/posts/the-us-supreme-court-ruled-5-4-monday-that-states-can-count-mail-in-ballots-that/1485180756810499/","title":"PBS NewsHour","description":"The U.S. Supreme Court ruled 5-4 Monday that states can count mail-in ballots that arrive after Election Day, rejecting a challenge led by President Donald Trump and his allies aimed at reining in...","language":"en","text":"The U.S. Supreme Court ruled 5-4 Monday that states can count mail-in ballots that arrive after Election Day, rejecting a challenge led by President Donald Trump and his allies aimed at reining in voting by mail.\n\n\"A Mississippi law permits the counting of absentee ballots postmarked by election day but received up to five days later,\" Justice Amy Coney Barrett wrote for the majority. \"We must decide whether the federal election-day statutes preempt Mississippi’s law. They do not.\"\n\nThe justices rejected the GOP-led push to revise laws in some states that allow mail-in ballots to be received and counted a prescribed number of days after the election, as long as the ballot is postmarked by Election Day. In more than half of the 50 states, those rules specifically apply to U.S. voters in the military and living overseas.\n\nHowever, Barrett wrote, \"this is not a case about the Constitution. We do not consider the scope of Congress’s authority to regulate federal elections. The sole question before us is whether counting ballots postmarked by Election Day, but received up to five days later, violates the federal election-day statutes.\"\n\nBarrett, Chief Justice John Roberts and Justices Sonia Sotomayor, Elena Kagan and Ketanji Brown Jackson made up the five-justice majority in the case, called Watson v. Republican National Committee. Justices Samuel Alito, Clarence Thomas, Neil Gorsuch and Brett Kavanaugh dissented.\n\nAll reactions:\n\n1.5K comments\n\n1.4K shares\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nMost relevant\n\n\n\nTerri Fawcett-Cromwell\n\nIt's crazy to think that 4 of them thought it was ok for the president to change a constitutional amendment himself.\n\n284\n\nView all 34 replies\n\nJack Franklin\n\nDidn't Trump vote by Mail? Why yes he did.\n\n322\n\nView all 51 replies\n\nJames Griffin\n\nMy question is why this decision was 5-4 and not unanimous or at least very close?!\n\n227\n\nView all 19 replies\n\nRalph Dean Ward\n\nThose 4 must love his taste. Taxes are fine being postmarked on taxday.\n\n113\n\nView all 4 replies\n\nJudy Olcott\n\nThe U.S. Postal Service is no longer postmarking mail when they receive it.\n\nIf anyone is planning to use mail-in ballots they need to take their ballots to a post office and request that they be hand postmarked.\n\nOtherwise their ballot may not be postmarked until after the election and their vote won’t count.\n\n60\n\nView all 11 replies\n\nMargot Dainowski\n\nI can’t believe it!! The Supreme Court did something right?!\n\n135\n\nView all 23 replies\n\nDaniel Killman\n\nI never understood ol’ Donny’s logic on this anyway. He seems to think this would have made things harder more for the Dems than for the GOP voters. I can’t see any evidence that that would be the case. I’d say that just as many Republicans depend on mail-in as Democrats.\n\n62\n\nView all 11 replies\n\nMark Moran\n\nThe fact that this wasn’t unanimous is concerning.\n\n723\n\nView all 85 replies\n\nReid Seibold\n\nA major loss for the Suppress American Voter Eligibility (save act), and a HUGE victory for American voters.\n\n63","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":2175.0214969997614,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}